<BaseLayout title="Pallet Conveyor Systems: CDLR, Chain, Roller Types &#x26; Load Capacity | PackagingConveyor.com" description="Pallet conveyor systems: CDLR, drag chain, roller, and turntable types. Load capacity, stretch wrap integration, and selection criteria for pallet handling." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Pallet Conveyor Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Pallet conveyors are heavy-duty conveyor systems that transport loaded and empty pallets through packaging, warehousing, and manufacturing operations. They use chain-driven live rollers (CDLR), drag chains, or heavy-duty powered rollers to move loads, from empty pallet stacks to heavy beverage and drum pallets, between palletizers, stretch wrappers, pallet labelers, staging areas, and shipping docks.</p>
<p>Pallet conveyors form the backbone of every end-of-line packaging system. They connect the palletizer output to the stretch wrapper, then route wrapped pallets to staging lanes or directly to truck loading positions.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Pallet Conveyor Types: CDLR, Drag Chain, Roller &amp; Turntable</h2>
<h3>Chain-Driven Live Roller (CDLR)</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> are the most common pallet conveyor type. Steel rollers mount in a heavy-gauge steel frame and connect through sprocket-and-chain drives powered by a single motor. The chain loop drives the rollers, which carry the pallet on their surface.</p>
<p>CDLR advantages include a wide load capacity range across models and compatibility with standard GMA pallets. As an example of that range, <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/Pallet-Handling-Brochure.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol's pallet handling catalog</a> rates its 199-CRR at up to 250 lb per linear foot, its 25-CRR at 1,000 lb per foot, and its 36-CRRH at 2,000 lb per foot. Accumulation on CDLR uses zones: each zone has its own drive or clutch, so a zone can stop under a pallet while the next zone keeps running.</p>
<p>Larger roller diameter, heavier tube wall, closer roller centers, and larger chain all raise capacity. Compare suppliers on rated load per linear foot and per zone, not on roller diameter alone.</p>
<h3>Drag Chain Conveyors</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">Drag chain</a> pallet conveyors use two or three strands of heavy-duty chain running in steel or UHMW tracks. Pallets ride directly on the chain strands. The chain is driven by sprockets at one end and idles on sprockets at the other end.</p>
<p>Drag chain suits non-standard pallet sizes, slave pallets, and applications where pallets are too damaged or irregular for reliable roller transport. Drag chain spreads the load along the chain strands rather than on individual roller bearings, which suits heavy loads. Hytrol, for example, rates its DC-60 and DC-63 drag chain conveyors at 3,000 lb per zone on #60 chain and its DC-82 and DC-83 at 4,000 lb per zone on #80 chain, with two strands on the DC-60 and DC-82 and three strands on the DC-63 and DC-83. The disadvantage: drag chain does not accumulate without separate zones, each with its own chain drive.</p>
<p>Drag chain is also common in warehouse systems where pallets transfer between AS/RS (Automated Storage and Retrieval Systems) and outbound staging.</p>
<h3>Heavy-Duty Powered Roller</h3>
<p>Heavy-duty powered roller conveyors use belt-driven or line-shaft-driven large-diameter rollers. They serve applications requiring higher speeds than CDLR or where chain maintenance is undesirable. Belt-driven roller conveyors generate less noise than chain-driven systems and require less lubrication.</p>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">Live roller conveyors</a> in heavy-duty configurations handle pallet loads using V-belt or round-belt drives between rollers. Load capacity depends on roller diameter, bearing rating, and belt tension.</p>
<h3>Turntable Conveyors</h3>
<p>Pallet turntables rotate pallets 90 or 180 degrees for direction changes in conveyor layouts. The turntable platform contains a short section of roller or chain conveyor that aligns with the incoming and outgoing conveyor lines. A motor or pneumatic actuator rotates the platform to the target angle.</p>
<p>Turntables handle direction changes where right-angle <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyors</a> cannot accommodate the pallet size or weight. Each turn is a stop, rotate, and restart cycle, which limits throughput compared with chain transfers. Hytrol's 26-CRPT turntable, for example, is rated to move a 4,000 lb pallet in 90-degree increments.</p>
<h3>Right-Angle Transfer (Chain Transfer)</h3>
<p>Chain transfer conveyors move pallets at 90-degree angles without rotation. Pop-up chain strands rise between the rollers of a CDLR conveyor, lift the pallet off the rollers, and carry it perpendicular to the original direction. Chain transfers handle higher throughput than turntables because there is no rotation cycle. Capacity varies widely by model: Hytrol's chain transfers range from the CT-200, rated for a 200 lb pallet, to the CT-6000, rated for 6,000 lb.</p>
<p>Right-angle transfers serve as routing points in pallet conveyor systems, directing pallets to different staging lanes, dock doors, or storage rows based on PLC logic.</p>
<h2 id="load-heading">Pallet Conveyor Load Capacity and Sizing</h2>
<p>Pallet conveyor load capacity depends on roller diameter, roller spacing (pitch), bearing rating, chain size, and frame construction. Every component in the conveyor must carry the maximum expected load with a safety margin. Guarding, emergency stops, and crossovers fall under <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>. Under-specifying any single component creates a weak link that limits the entire system.</p>
